    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe new step is placed, named, wired, and documented correctly \u2014 `flagCityCapacityWarning` mirrors the existing `flagBulkSignup`/`flagPossibleDuplicate` steps, and the model/mapper/schema/OpenAPI additions all follow the `bulkSignupWarning` precedent exactly. One structural issue:\n\n**Duplicated city-count predicate \u2014 should reuse a shared helper, mirroring `OwnerDailyCount`.**\n\n`FlagCityCapacityWarning.service` copies the city-counting logic from `RejectCityAtCapacity` verbatim:\n\n```java\nString city = owner.getCity() == null ? \"\" : owner.getCity().trim();\nlong count = ownerRepository.findAll().stream()\n        .filter(existing -> existing.getCity() != null\n                && city.equalsIgnoreCase(existing.getCity().trim()))\n        .count();\n```\n\nThis is the same reject-at-hard-limit / flag-when-approaching pair that already exists for registration dates: `RejectOverDailyLimit` (\u2265100) and `FlagBulkSignup` (>80) both key off the count, and the codebase deliberately puts the predicate in one place \u2014 `OwnerDailyCount.sharingRegistrationDate(...)` \u2014 whose javadoc states the intent: *\"Both `RejectOverDailyLimit` and `FlagBulkSignup` key off this count at different thresholds; keeping the predicate here avoids maintaining it in two places.\"*\n\nThe change under review introduces exactly the two-places duplication that established convention avoids. The city-matching rule (null-safe, trimmed, case-insensitive) now lives in both `RejectCityAtCapacity` and `FlagCityCapacityWarning`; a future change to how cities are matched must be made in both, and the new class's javadoc even has to assert it counts \"the same way `RejectCityAtCapacity` counts\" \u2014 a comment that only exists because the logic was copied rather than shared.\n\nSuggested fix: extract an `OwnerCityCount` helper (analogous to `OwnerDailyCount`) with a package-private `static long sharingCity(OwnerRepository, String city)`, and have both `RejectCityAtCapacity` and `FlagCityCapacityWarning` call it. That keeps the two thresholds (`CITY_CAPACITY = 50`, `WARN_THRESHOLD = 40`) in their respective steps while the matching predicate has a single home.",
